L000130696525000011_a6-kannualreportandaccounts,rfs,1,flag,,,"I also went to China where I delivered a lecture at Tsinghua University School of Economics and Management, and presented awards to the finalists of the Shell Eco-marathon"
L000130696525000011_a6-kannualreportandaccounts,rfs,1,flag,,,"As noted in last year's report, during 2023, the tender process for the appointment of the external auditor was commenced, with a view to reaching a conclusion during 2024"
L000130696525000011_a6-kannualreportandaccounts,term_years,3.0,years,,,"The ARC also considered the merits of extending the viability statement beyond a period of three years and concluded that the three-year period selected by the Board for the review of Shell's prospects, in line with the Operating Plan, remained appropriate"
L000130696525000011_a6-kannualreportandaccounts,term_years,3.0,years,,,"Particularly noting that assumptions do go well beyond the three-year period and do take into account climate change and energy transition, the ARC supported the going concern basis of accounting and the inclusion of Shell's viability statement in ""Other regulatory and statutory information"" on page 217"
L000130696525000011_a6-kannualreportandaccounts,rfs,1,flag,,,"The tender process, led by the ARC, was commenced during 2023 and completed in 2024"
L000130696525000011_a6-kannualreportandaccounts,usd_other,16500.0,$M,,,"In 2024, Shell delivered another set of strong financial results, with income of $16.5 billion, cash flow from operations (CFFO) of $54.7 billion, our second best year on record, and free cash flow* (FCF) of $39.5 billion"
L000130696525000011_a6-kannualreportandaccounts,usd_other,54700.0,$M,,,"In 2024, Shell delivered another set of strong financial results, with income of $16.5 billion, cash flow from operations (CFFO) of $54.7 billion, our second best year on record, and free cash flow* (FCF) of $39.5 billion"
L000130696525000011_a6-kannualreportandaccounts,usd_other,39500.0,$M,,,"In 2024, Shell delivered another set of strong financial results, with income of $16.5 billion, cash flow from operations (CFFO) of $54.7 billion, our second best year on record, and free cash flow* (FCF) of $39.5 billion"
L000130696525000011_a6-kannualreportandaccounts,rfs,1,flag,,,"In 2024, Shell delivered another set of strong financial results, with income of $16.5 billion, cash flow from operations (CFFO) of $54.7 billion, our second best year on record, and free cash flow* (FCF) of $39.5 billion"
L000130696525000011_a6-kannualreportandaccounts,contract_value,3100.0,$M,,,"We set four financial targets at CMD23, and have made progress against each of them, including reducing structural costs* by $3.1 billion since 2022 and delivering total shareholder distributions* of 41% of CFFO for the year, at the upper end of the 30-40% of CFFO range set out at CMD23"
L000130696525000011_a6-kannualreportandaccounts,annual_revenue,3900.0,$M,,,"In Marketing, we achieved the strongest annual Adjusted Earnings* since 2020 at $3.9 billion"
L000130696525000011_a6-kannualreportandaccounts,usd_other,54700.0,$M,,,"Finalising the 2024 pay outcomes In finalising pay outcomes, the REMCO considered Shell's wider performance and context during 2024 and over the LTIP performance period, paying particular attention to: ￮ The strong financial performance in 2024, with CFFO of $54.7 billion and FCF* of $39.5 billion, which has enabled Shell to invest in its businesses, reduce debt and enhance shareholder distributions. ￮ The shareholder experience, including cash dividends of $8.7 billion and share repurchases of $13.9 billion in 2024, resulting in total shareholder distributions* of $22.6 billion for the year and $71.4 billion over the LTIP performance period (2022-2024), and absolute and relative total shareholder return (TSR) performance over the same periods. ￮ Shell's performance beyond the formulaic outcomes of the variable pay structures, including safety (and fatalities), reputation, ethics and compliance, and feedback from the Audit and Risk Committee (ARC) and the Sustainability Committee (SUSCO). ￮ Shareholders' views on remuneration matters, as shared with the REMCO during engagements in March 2024, including positive feedback on the safety framework. ￮ The employee experience, where the REMCO noted the above- target vesting outcome for 2024 under the Performance Share Plan (PSP), used to make discretionary share awards below Senior Executive level and the average employee salary increases. ￮ Comparisons between the 2024 outcomes and historical remuneration levels. ￮ The alignment of the 10-year average outcomes of the annual bonus scorecard (1.13) and LTIP (96% of target) to the target level, demonstrating the effective design of the structures and the integrity of the target-setting process"
L000130696525000011_a6-kannualreportandaccounts,usd_other,39500.0,$M,,,"Finalising the 2024 pay outcomes In finalising pay outcomes, the REMCO considered Shell's wider performance and context during 2024 and over the LTIP performance period, paying particular attention to: ￮ The strong financial performance in 2024, with CFFO of $54.7 billion and FCF* of $39.5 billion, which has enabled Shell to invest in its businesses, reduce debt and enhance shareholder distributions. ￮ The shareholder experience, including cash dividends of $8.7 billion and share repurchases of $13.9 billion in 2024, resulting in total shareholder distributions* of $22.6 billion for the year and $71.4 billion over the LTIP performance period (2022-2024), and absolute and relative total shareholder return (TSR) performance over the same periods. ￮ Shell's performance beyond the formulaic outcomes of the variable pay structures, including safety (and fatalities), reputation, ethics and compliance, and feedback from the Audit and Risk Committee (ARC) and the Sustainability Committee (SUSCO). ￮ Shareholders' views on remuneration matters, as shared with the REMCO during engagements in March 2024, including positive feedback on the safety framework. ￮ The employee experience, where the REMCO noted the above- target vesting outcome for 2024 under the Performance Share Plan (PSP), used to make discretionary share awards below Senior Executive level and the average employee salary increases. ￮ Comparisons between the 2024 outcomes and historical remuneration levels. ￮ The alignment of the 10-year average outcomes of the annual bonus scorecard (1.13) and LTIP (96% of target) to the target level, demonstrating the effective design of the structures and the integrity of the target-setting process"
